we are  black folks in this country that are all  we're doing  is saying our lives matter we're not  asking for more than somebody else is  asking for  we want our children to grow up we want  to survive we want to have the same  quality of life  welcome back to cbs this morning that's  missouri congresswoman corey bush  reacting to the conviction of derek  chovin for the murder of george floyd  bush got into politics  in response to the 2014 death of  18-year-old michael brown  who was killed by a ferguson missouri  police officer  president obama's justice department  found darren wilson  shot brown in self-defense the massive  protests in the city also  inspired others to get involved in the  streets and in government  including fran griffin our national  correspondent jerica duncan watched  yesterday's chauvin verdict in ferguson  with the former activist turned city  councilwoman  when law reads the verdict says they  will appear on the permanent record  unintentional second degree murder while  committing a felony  find the defendant guilty it took a  second for the word guilty to sink in  perpetrating an eminently dangerous act  find the defendant guilty  creating an unreasonable risk find the  defendant guilty  but after hearing it three times tears  of joy  are these your true and cried and a  moment to herself  even take it for a minute as we waited  at the kitchen table  griffin sobbed from her bedroom when she  returned she told us why  april 20 2021 changed the history  of this nation we got to hold someone  accountable  a police officer who killed  an unarmed black man  he was held accountable today to me  it's kind of shocking because i never  seen nobody  actually guilty what does this mean for  your sons  your two black sons if anything it gives  them  confidence that they can actually create  the change minutes after the verdict the  text messages and  calls poured in phone's buzzing you want  to answer it yeah  hello guilty on all charges yes  griffin says she wasn't sure what to  expect  in 2014 she stood with protesters in  ferguson  after then officer darren wilson shot  and killed michael brown  a mile from her home no probable cause  exists to file any charge against  officer wilson  wilson and other witnesses testified  brown punched him in the face  before he shot brown when a grand jury  decided not to indict wilson  griffin watched as parts of her city  went up in flames  in 2019 she defeated a long-term  incumbent to become the city's first  black councilwoman to represent the  third ward  griffin has been on the front lines  pushing for more police training  and accountability from officers she  hopes yesterday's verdict  is not the exception every time we go  through this process  like it  it's traumatizing it's triggering  you don't know which way it's going to  go everybody's holding their breath  for them to find him  guilty on all three charges it does give  you  just a few moments to take a breath  then we gotta finish working  griffin who is the mother of three has a  14 year old an 18 year old and 21 year  old said yesterday was also about  showing the youth  what is possible when it comes to  creating change saying quote  the younger they get the message the  harder they will fight  to achieve it and i also just want to  mention the symbolism  yesterday it was snowing it was freezing  out here  and when we left miss fran's home the  sun  had come out also she has a cute little  dog that  had was in other rooms before the  verdict came out and right before  moments before that little dog came  running in almost  as if the dog even knew that something  big was about to happen and was coming  wow yeah rodrigo that's an amazing  conversation incredible to capture the  moment we've seen the jubilation the  cheers and with fran  the quiet recognition to see it slowly  sink in  slowly sinking very powerful stuff  edrica thank you very much

